plan Machines
title   Machines: definitions, fragments, states, turns, guards, schedules, activities and reactions
status  proposed
goal
    An author declares an actor's states and events once. Each accepted event produces one consistent result; guards,
    views and tests agree about what the actor can do.

    **Why now:** everything that runs or reads an actor depends on one meaning for its states and events, so the
    declaration and the turn algorithm are fixed once, here.

    **Read first:** @spec.Compile and @spec.Turn, the Specs built from this Plan.
outcomes
    O1  Definitions, fragments and machines
        Declaring a definition or machine starts nothing, and an invalid declaration throws one FlowError listing every
        violation found.

        definition(config) and machine(definition, callback) return synchronously and start no actors, work, timers or
        storage. machine() is the compile: its output is plain data plus named closures, booted by the Runtime in the
        same process (@plan.Runtime.O1).

        Invalid construction throws one FlowError containing all discovered violations in declaration then rule order,
        with offending field paths. The callback runs exactly once with { S, E, O } and returns static
        machine configuration.

        A definition requires id, states and events, and may declare `internalEvents`, memory, traits, operations,
        context, `props`, `onProps`, `emits` and persist. persist defaults to false; persist: true on the definition
        makes the machine's shared actors persisted (@plan.Persistence.O2). states is an ordered list of leaf names,
        single-key compounds containing child lists, or { NAME: Sub } placing a subroutine; a node that carries traits
        is wrapped as Flow.state(children | {} | Sub, { traits }). events and optional `internalEvents` are records from
        event names to Schema field records; {} means no payload.

        A payload may instead be a Schema.Struct with checks; a payload failing its check is refused at admission,
        before guards run.

        An internal event may be marked Flow.uncertain(fields); every unknown: target of an operation is such an event
        or Flow.ignore (@plan.Operations.O3), otherwise UnknownNotUncertain at construction.

        Memory is a record of Schema fields; a field's start is written on the field. Flow.input(s) is supplied by the
        creator, and the definition's Input type is exactly these fields; Flow.initial(s, value) is a constant start and
        Flow.initial(s, ({ ...fields }) => value) a pure computed start from input or constant fields, never from
        another computed field; Flow.private(s) is seen only by its owner.

        Every field starts from exactly one source: Flow.input, Flow.initial, or absence for an optional key; a field
        with none is a type error, and Flow.input and Flow.initial never share a field. A field has one default, the
        same at creation and restore; two different defaults, or a Schema default beside Flow.initial, are TwoDefaults,
        found from the Schema's structure.

        `props` is a record of Schema fields that the outside world sets and the machine only reads. Every prop has a
        value before any use, its Schema's default; how the Schema declares it is the author's choice. Props are never
        persisted or transferred, and only local actors take them (`useActor`, `createActor`, `Story.machine`); a shared
        actor's outside configuration is context or input. `emits` is a record from emit names to Schema field records;
        {} means no payload.

        A default is pure: a default whose Effect is not a succeeded value (a clock read, a fresh id per call) is a
        construction error, ImpureDefault (proposed name, the runner's), found by reading the Schema without running it;
        a time or an id comes from an event or the Runtime's mint. Flow is outermost: a Schema modifier over a Flow
        marker is a type error.

        Creation is pure and runs once per incarnation: it checks a present input by the Input Schema's type side, with
        no transforming decode, copies the input fields, applies constant then computed initials, and reads no clock or
        randomness, because it also runs in render (@plan.React.O2). It never runs on restore, adoption or hydration.

        Input fields are ordinary memory afterwards. A machine's input fields are read-only (writing one is a type
        error), since they name the actor (@plan.Runtime.O3); on a value-named machine they are required, default-free
        and string-encoded, else IdentityFieldOptional (proposed name).

        A fragment's input fields stay writable. At restore a field the record lacks takes its default or computed
        initial, an optional key stays absent, and an input field with no default refuses the record
        (RestoreMissingInput); record versions, resets and dropped fields are @plan.Persistence.O5.

        Optional context maps slots to another definition's Provider.select(selector); context binding is
        @plan.Runtime.O5. operations is ({ E }) => ({ name: contract.outcomes({ ... }) | contract }): each server
        interaction is declared once by name with its endings mapped onto this definition's events (@plan.Operations.O1
        to O3); one write operation per write contract per actor, counting every fragment placement in its tree
        (SharedContract, proposed name, otherwise).

        The definition exports O, the operations' handles; a commit's or lookup's select is typed at its transition.
        Machine configuration mirrors the declared state tree exactly: root and compounds require a default direct-child
        token and states; leaves have neither.

        Nodes may declare on, activities and schedules, and fill each placed fragment in one block (traits under
        traits.name where placed, a subroutine on its state); only the root may declare `onContext` and `onProps`. A
        definition's handler(path, h) and guard(path?, g) type shared code as if written at path (the root
        when omitted).

        Fragments. trait(declaration, body) is something the actor has; subroutine(declaration, body) is a state the
        actor goes into.

        A fragment declares id, events, `hostEvents` (raised only by its direct host), `internalEvents` (from its own
        schedules or operation endings), guards (Flow.Guard), callbacks (Flow.Callback), exits (Flow.Exit, subroutines
        only), memory, emits, child traits, operations and, for a subroutine, states and default; ports are required
        unless wrapped in Flow.optional (an optional guard allows, an optional callback ignores). Its body holds on,
        schedules, activities, its children's fillings and `whenAbandoned` (notified when an incarnation ends other than
        by its own exit or a freeze).

        Each fragment carries an inferred summary (per event: callbacks notified, exits taken, emits, totality;
        whenAbandoned's callbacks; its and its children's write operations and their Effect requirements), part of its
        type; hosts are checked against summaries, never bodies. Fragments may not declare context, `onContext`,
        `props`, `onProps`, send or persist; a fragment is configured by input and host events.

        Placement:

        - the root's traits, a node's Flow.state traits, or a subroutine state;
        - T.resumable() freezes instead of dropping when its node is left;
        - T.each({ of, max, by? }) places one copy per key of a host memory field.

        A `resetOn` filling returns a reset value compared by Equal.equals; null or undefined means the trait is not
        placed (becoming absent ends the incarnation and runs whenAbandoned, becoming present starts one), and the
        filling's parts receive key, the present reset value (the copy key under .each).

        A raise into a trait placed with .each reaches every copy live at turn start, in key order. A copy with no
        handler on its active path is skipped with `Unhandled` evidence; a copy whose guards deny refuses the whole
        turn; with no live copies the raise does nothing and is recorded. Keys past `max` are dropped with `listRepair`
        evidence, never refused.

        Trait instance names, operation names, state names, memory keys, prop names and emit names share one namespace;
        a collision rejects; key and item are reserved memory names in a trait placed with .each.

        A fragment's operations map onto its own `internalEvents`. A fragment may own a write that maps anything only if
        every placement from the actor's root to it is a root trait, an .each copy or a .resumable() trait, any of which
        may carry `resetOn`, with no subroutine on the path; there a `resetOn` must return a string, null or undefined.

        Elsewhere it may own reads, streams and bare writes. Acting members (commit, lookup, subscribe, retry, cancel,
        cancelAll, refetch) are typed only in the declaring fragment; outside it Fragment.O offers only its write
        operations' reads (get in views; get, idle and retryable in a host's guards and updateMemory).

        A key-less O.x.cancel listed in `whenAbandoned` releases that operation's lanes of the ending incarnation on
        leave (@plan.Operations.O7).

        Unknown fields, wrong kinds, empty compounds, duplicate siblings, overlapping public and internal event names,
        _tag payload/input/memory fields, self-selecting context and invalid operation handles reject. State names is
        and child, top-level root, and the names self and __proto__ (ReservedStateName) are reserved.

        State paths have at most ten segments. Authored names and ids are nonempty, at most 256 UTF-8 bytes, contain no
        C0 controls, DEL or lone surrogates, and retain exact spelling: no Unicode normalization or locale comparison.

        Construction errors also include unfilled required ports, a raise into a fragment the turn's state change exits,
        two state changers or overlapping keys that state-token pruning cannot separate, an acting fragment member used
        outside its fragment, and a mapped write or `resetOn` outside the fragment write scope above; the full finding
        list is @plan.CommandLine.O5.

        The machine cannot add states, events or operations. Foreign tokens, undeclared handler events, foreign producer
        events, non-public send events and duplicate schedule names reject. Targets, operations, outcome events and
        send/write targets are static; callbacks compute values only. Starting a write is static: only by listing a
        commit in actions.

        Definition literals, payloads, context, operation names and state paths infer without assertions, explicit
        generics or wrappers; invalid machine additions are also type errors. Definitions expose Event, State, Input,
        Memory and Snapshot Schemas.

        Event decodes only public _tag envelopes; State accepts complete leaf paths. E.X(payload) validates on every
        call and throws InvalidEvent on mismatch; payload-free constructors take no argument. Persisted-value
        eligibility is @plan.Persistence.O2.
    O2  States and transitions
        Exactly one leaf is active. Substates share the actor's memory, context and lifetime; reaching a leaf with no
        handlers does not complete or dispose the actor. S.OPEN.EDITING names that state; token.is(snapshot or state)
        includes descendants and narrows types.

        A state token may be an element of a guard array; it passes when the pre-turn state is at or below it, and flow
        check reads it structurally. token.child(snapshot) returns its active direct child's name, otherwise undefined.
        S.root.child(snapshot) returns the active top-level state. S.X.history targets X's last active child, or its
        default, and S.X.history.child(s) reads it; history restores control only, and data comes back only through
        placement or .resumable().

        on maps an event name to a token, { target?, guard?, updateMemory?, actions?, raise?, notify?, emit? }, or an
        ordered array of transitions. target is this machine's token or "self"; an omitted target means "self". The
        active leaf's handler is tried first, then ancestors up to the root.

        At each level, the first passing or unguarded alternative wins; all declining falls through to the next
        ancestor. With no enabled transition, nothing changes and no work starts. A child's refusal inside a selected
        alternative never falls through (@O3).

        `emit: [emits.X(payload)]` tells the actor's listeners something once. Any accepted transition emits: a send, a
        firing, an ending, an `onContext` or `onProps` event. Entry, exit and a refused turn never do. A copy that ends
        in the same turn still delivers. Delivery is @plan.React.O3; emits are never stored, persisted, transferred
        or replayed.

        "self" changes no state, restarts no work and keeps the activation, including at the root. An explicit target
        re-enters itself if active, as a new activation, even when it names the handler's own state, so a call the old
        activation owns ends late (@plan.Operations.O3); targeting an active ancestor re-enters it and its descendants
        through its defaults.

        For a handler at H targeting T, the unchanged boundary is H when T is a strict descendant of H; otherwise it is
        the nearest proper ancestor of T that also contains H. Active states below that boundary exit deepest first; the
        path to T and its defaults enters outermost first.

        Work at and above that boundary survives unchanged. Parents' activities start before children's and stop after
        them. Defaults reach a leaf in one publication with no intermediate work or snapshot.

        A subroutine state is entered at its default, or is a leaf. It is left by an exit, or abandoned by a transition
        that exits it; host events fall through to its ancestors as usual, and an ancestor's "self" does not abandon it.
        Exits are targets only fragments take; an exit filling must leave the subroutine's state, and every exit is
        filled. A callback never moves the host out of the fragment's placement node.
    O3  Turns
        Each accepted event, with everything it raises, notifies, exits, resets and leaves, compiles into one turn that
        the runtime and check() run by the same algorithm.

        Input ordering and atomic actor/store publication are @plan.Runtime.O4. The turn algorithm is normative for the
        runtime and for check(): admit, select the source, select the parts, seed and compute, validate, commit. One
        transition and everything it raises, notifies, exits, resets and leaves compile into one turn.

        Admit (runtime only): a pending reset from the last commit runs first as its own follow-up turn; otherwise the
        next event. A call's ending is mapped here through its operation's outcomes, its lane cell applied, and its
        owner tested against the pre-turn snapshot (@plan.Operations.O3); a timer of a dead activation is discarded;
        firings with the same deadline are separate turns. A props update is its own turn: it changes only props, then
        runs `onProps` (@O7).

        Select the source: from the active leaf, or for a late ending the ancestor its owner test names, try
        alternatives in order, state-token elements first.

        When no state from the routed one up to the root writes a handler for an event, the deepest active state whose
        schedule lists it in `armOn` or `disarmOn` handles it with an implicit unguarded { target: "self" }; a written
        handler at any level is selected first. If every alternative declines, continue at the next ancestor; after the
        root the event is Denied (a handler existed) or Unhandled, and nothing of the turn's own changes, though an
        ending admitted at step one keeps its lane effects, counts as delivered and re-checks `keys` and `paused` (@O6).

        Select the parts: walk raises, the child transitions they select, the notifies and fillings these reach, and
        exits, depth first in written order; every selected alternative fixes its target. Before the walk `resetOn` runs
        and changed traits get a new incarnation, or end if absent. If an addressed child has no handler or all its
        alternatives decline, the part refuses the whole turn. A raise into a trait placed with .each is the one
        exception (@O1).

        After the walk each .each field's final list decides membership, and `whenAbandoned` runs for every incarnation
        the turn ends, fused into the leaving turn. Validate: list repair (never refuses), one state changer among the
        parts, disjoint memory keys, the after memory, a valid action batch, then each write member's lane cell in
        action order and the turn's releases.

        Commit: exits deepest first and entries outermost first, publish the after memory, cancel or freeze ended
        incarnations' schedules, arm schedules, re-evaluate `resetOn` into pending resets, queue adoption deliveries of
        started incarnations, publish once.

        Two memory views. Before, the pre-turn snapshot, is read by every guard, every payload mapper (raise, notify,
        emit, exits, whenAbandoned) and every `updateMemory`. Every reader of memory receives props from the
        same snapshot.

        After, the memory the turn publishes (new incarnations' initial memory, then every patch in expansion order,
        after list repair), is read by work that only starts or times things: commit and lookup selects, member key
        selects, schedule functions, keys, `restartOn`, `paused`, placement selects and when, and optimistic (captured
        at start). `resetOn` and input fillers read the before-walk memory (the pre-turn memory with the source's patch
        and earlier resets).

        Lane reads (operations, get, idle, retryable) in guards, selects and `updateMemory` see the pre-turn lanes with
        the turn's own ending admitted; keys, when, placement selects and published rows see the lanes with this turn's
        members applied. Each Partial<Memory> patch shallow-merges once. Only accepted transitions change memory.
        Updates, action parameters and send mappers receive now, one Runtime Clock epoch millisecond value shared
        throughout the turn; guards receive no time.

        One invalid action, a failing after-memory select or an invalid duration (@O5) rejects the whole turn like a
        pre-commit defect: prior state and memory stay, no action runs, and one issue-only snapshot names the code;
        check() reports it as Denied with code InvalidTurn.

        A value that cannot be encoded never rejects a turn (@plan.Operations.O3). After publication, exited work
        releases, entered work starts, then finite actions start or join, invoked after the turn. Unhandled or denied
        events publish nothing, change no revision and start no work; denials may be observed in evidence without
        a revision.

        A pre-commit callback defect preserves prior state, memory, context and store truth, starts no work and
        publishes one issue-only snapshot naming the callback and path. It is not retried for that input. This includes
        guards, updates, selects, keys, send and emit payloads, schedules, `restartOn`, `paused`, placement selects and
        when predicates.

        A post-commit mapper, placeholder, optimistic-data or write callback defect publishes one issue, enqueues no
        event and applies no partial write batch; it neither rolls back the committed state nor retries that occurrence.
        Write semantics are @plan.Operations.O5.

        Outside callers can send only valid public event envelopes; invalid payloads or internal events fail with
        InvalidEvent before a turn exists. The same snapshot, event, Clock and seeded test randomness produce the same
        result without consulting host wall time.
    O4  Guards, deny and check
        Guards decide from the pre-turn snapshot and start no work, so views can trust check.

        A guard receives { event, state, memory, props, context, schedules, operations } from the pre-turn snapshot and
        returns true or deny(code, context?). code is a literal string; context is optional JSON-safe explanatory data.
        deny returns plain data matching Flow.Denial, so a denial may be stored in memory and a guard may return a
        stored one. false is a type error; any invalid return is a guard defect.

        Guards read only these captured inputs and start no work, so views can trust check. A guard slot may hold an
        array; all must pass. An operation's idle(k?) (a partial key passes only when every lane it matches is Idle) and
        retryable(k?, { times }) are guards (@plan.Operations.O3); O.x.get(operations, k) reads a row.

        A host reads a fragment's write lanes with Fragment.O.x.get, idle and retryable; every `updateMemory` receives
        operations (pre-turn lanes, get only); inside a fragment operations is its own rows.

        check(snapshot, event) runs the turn's selection, parts and validation without updating memory, admitting
        events, publishing, recording evidence, minting, joining queues, predicting, marking releases or starting work.
        It returns { _tag: "Allowed" } if a transition would run; { _tag: "Denied", reasons } if handlers exist but all
        decline, or the turn would be rejected (code InvalidTurn); or { _tag: "Unhandled" } if none exists.

        Denied reasons contain { code, context } for each declined alternative, deepest level then authored order; a
        declined state-token element reads { code: "NotInState", context: { state } }.

        A decline from another subject path is a verdict, like Rejected. Verdicts compare by Equal.equals. An old
        snapshot answers from its own captured values.

        A defective guard makes check throw GuardDefect without publishing an issue. Internal or foreign events are type
        errors and fail untyped calls with InvalidEvent.
    O5  Schedules
        A schedule fires an event on a declared timing, started and stopped by the events the machine lists.

        A node or fragment body declares schedules: { name: { schedule, send, armOn?, disarmOn?, restartOn?, keys?,
        paused? } }. schedule is a Duration.Input, an Effect Schedule, or ({ key?, memory, props, context, now }) =>
        either, reading the after memory of its arming turn with that turn's now. Every function of a keyed schedule
        (`schedule`, `paused`, `restartOn`, `send`) receives `key`, which is identity only.

        A fixed negative duration is a construction error. A computed zero or negative delay counts as zero: due at
        once, fired once on the next turn, never in the arming turn. A computed NaN, infinite or over 2^31 - 1 ms delay
        rejects the arming turn with the pre-commit defect behavior of @O3. send is this definition's payload-free event
        or E.X.from(metadata); metadata contains name, attempt (firings since arming, starting at 1), `armedAt`,
        `dueAt`, `firedAt`, output and, for a keyed schedule, key. Times use Runtime Clock milliseconds; Duration output
        is undefined. Armed schedules expose `{ dueAt }` while running and `{ left }` while paused, never both, and
        `pausedBy` beside `left`; `left` is milliseconds on the Runtime Clock (@O8). An event mapped by an operation is
        never a schedule's send.

        A schedule without `armOn` starts on entry. `armOn` and `disarmOn` list event tokens, `armOn` also `Flow.entry`.
        armOn: [...] starts it on each accepted listed event, raised ones included, accepted anywhere on the active
        path, restarting it if running; with Flow.entry in the list it also starts on entry (its owner's state entered,
        trait created or reset, never a thaw), otherwise it is idle on entry. disarmOn: [events] stops it on each
        accepted listed event; stopping an idle schedule does nothing.

        Exit stops any schedule. An event in both lists is a construction error; only `armOn: [Flow.entry]` may sit
        beside `restartOn`, and any other `armOn` with `restartOn` is a construction error; a turn that does both arms
        once, from step one. Every temporal construct is delayed: it never counts the turn that arms it. Descendant
        changes and "self" preserve deadlines. Each arming has an id: a firing from an earlier arming, or from an
        activation that ended before handling, is never delivered.

        A Duration fires once; a Schedule fires each step until complete. Equal deadlines enqueue by actor admission
        order, then outermost state, then declaration order, each a separate turn. An overdue step fires once, never in
        a burst; the next delay starts at actual firing.

        restartOn({ key?, memory, props, context }) establishes a silent baseline from the activating turn's after
        memory and leaves the schedule idle on entry unless `armOn` lists Flow.entry. After each committed turn, a
        changed selection under Equal.equals restarts only that schedule from its first step at commit time; equal
        values do nothing. A Duration returns to idle after firing, allowing debounce behavior. Restarting drops any
        time left.

        `paused: { Reason: ({ key?, memory, props, context, operations }) => boolean }` names each reason; any true
        pauses the timer; the snapshot shows `pausedBy`, the active reason names in declaration order, beside `left`. A
        turn runs keys, then `restartOn`, then `paused`, after each committed turn. Every lane change, including a
        denied or unhandled ending's admission, re-checks `keys` and `paused`. True holds the time left of the current
        delay (`left = dueAt - now`, at least 0); false resumes from it (`dueAt = now + left`). Paused time never counts
        toward a delay; an unused actor, a frozen trait and a closed page still count. Arming or restarting while paused
        holds the full delay. A pause right after a firing holds the next step's full delay. A multi-step Schedule
        pauses inside its current delay, and its `attempt` does not advance. `disarmOn`, exit or a key leaving removes a
        paused timer; nothing fires later.

        A keyed schedule's keys return key records from the after memory and after lanes (inside a fragment its own
        rows; under .each that copy's lanes): one timer per key, armed when the key appears, cancelled when it leaves,
        firing once with key; a repeated key keeps its first position with `listRepair` evidence; `restartOn` is
        evaluated per key, ({ key, memory, props }) => value; `armOn` on a keyed schedule is a construction error.

        Per-key timers persist by key with `dueAt` or `left`, and on restore the restored memory's keys win.

        Schedules fire only while their actor is used (@plan.Runtime.O3). A deadline that comes due while nothing uses
        the actor waits, and only the latest arming waits (re-arming, disarming or exiting removes a waiting deadline).

        On restore, resume, a return after an end, or a use returning to an actor whose schedules waited, a Duration
        preserves its deadline and fires once if overdue, waiting deadlines in deadline order; a paused timer keeps its
        `left`. A multi-step Schedule restarts at step one; a paused one first finishes its `left`. The host steps a
        Schedule and hands the core the next delay as data. A resumable trait's schedules keep `dueAt` or `left` while
        frozen and fire once on return if overdue. Restored `restartOn` baselines are silent, and `keys` and `paused`
        are re-evaluated silently on restore, hydration and return. Hydration activation is @plan.ServerRendering.O5;
        deterministic time is @plan.Testing.O2.
    O6  Activities and outcomes in machines
        Operation activities are placed in states and traits, and their work is kept, replaced or released as the
        machine changes state.

        activities is a list of placements: O.x.subscribe(select, { when? }) or O.x.subscribe.each(select, { when? })
        (one session per distinct key of the selected inputs, repaired like .each keys), declared in a state (a
        subroutine's states included) or a trait body and placed for that activation or incarnation. select and when
        read the after memory and after lanes; when may hold state tokens.

        Lane shapes and the placement's placeholder option are @plan.Operations.O6, streams @plan.Operations.O4. After
        each turn, an unchanged operation and key keep existing work; a changed key releases old work exactly once and
        starts new work; when false releases only that placement. A freeze releases a trait's placements and a thaw
        places them again.

        A live operation is placed in one state or trait incarnation at a time unless state tokens in when make
        placements exclusive (DuplicatePlacement); one operation uses one verb (VerbMismatch). A stream placement is one
        session per (actor incarnation, placement path, operation, key). Release failure reports once without blocking
        the transition.

        Endings map to events on the declarer, never at the call site: each operation's outcomes name the events
        (@plan.Operations.O2, @plan.Operations.O3, @plan.Operations.O4). Mapped events run in later ordinary turns.
        value fires only for a changed confirmed value under Equal.equals, never for predictions or optimistic values.

        Lane-only changes publish captured lanes without running handlers, guards, updates, actions, schedule functions,
        `restartOn`, `onContext` or `onProps`, except that every lane change re-checks `keys` and `paused` (@O5); a
        defect there publishes one issue and keeps the previous values, and the lane change still publishes. Finite work
        starts only from an accepted transition's actions; operation action semantics are @plan.Operations.O2 and
        @plan.Operations.O3 and sending is @plan.Runtime.O4.

        A commit, lookup or retry is owned by the activation of the state its transition names ("self": the handler's
        own state). At admission its ending is current, routed from the active leaf, if that activation is alive;
        otherwise late, routed from the owner's nearest surviving strict ancestor; an adopted ending and every unknown {
        Restored } are current from the active leaf.

        In a late turn an alternative may not exit a state that owns other pending work, or an active subroutine
        (LateMayNotLeave): pending work is every call owned by an activation it exits, an InFlight head and every queued
        commit, behind a Held head too, whatever the transition cancels; a Held head is not.

        A late handler gives work up in an alternative that stays put, with `cancelAll` or a keyed cancel. flow check
        errors on a late handler with no alternative that stays put at the owner state or its ancestors; a late ending
        landing while the owner is not re-entered, at an ancestor whose alternatives all leave, is denied at run time,
        an accepted risk (the runner's ruling).

        Lateness is not transitive, and only the host's own operations count. Every late event that can occur is handled
        at a strict ancestor of its owner.
    O7  Context reactions
        A change in context or props can raise an event into an actor, and a reaction never changes state directly.

        Machine-level onContext: [{ select, when?, event }] observes context changes. select({ context, memory })
        establishes a silent baseline at admission, restore, adoption and hydration. Only context waves re-run it, using
        current memory; memory-only changes do not trigger it. Context propagation is @plan.Runtime.O5. Equal selections
        under Equal.equals are silent.

        A changed selection becomes the baseline even if when(current, previous) declines. With no when, or when true,
        event (bare or E.X.from(current)) enqueues after the context turn, in reaction declaration order; `onContext`
        events queue in the actor's one FIFO mailbox with user sends, call endings and timer firings (@plan.Runtime.O4).

        An actor returning after an end within a Runtime gets one wave if a provider's selection differs from the one it
        ended with; a reload's baselines are silent. A select, when or mapper defect publishes one issue, keeps the
        previous baseline and emits nothing; the next wave may try again. Reactions never change state directly.

        Machine-level onProps: [{ select, when?, event }] is shaped like onContext, with select({ props, memory }). A
        props update is its own turn that changes only props; onProps then runs, so a reaction can repair memory against
        shrinking props. Its baseline is silent at admission; equal selections under Equal.equals are silent; defects
        behave as for onContext; its events queue in the one FIFO mailbox. Props reuse context's delivery; only the
        source differs: the owner at the call site, never saved.
    O8  The snapshot
        A snapshot is deeply frozen plain data showing state, memory, props, context, issues, lifecycle, schedules
        and operations.

        A snapshot exposes state (complete leaf path), memory (decoded value; fragment slices under their instance
        names, undefined while inactive and present while frozen, .each copies as a keyed list in key order, each with
        key), props (current values), context (selected slots), issues (Diagnostic values), lifecycle (active, disposing
        or disposed; a persisted identity's record that does not decode reads quarantined, @plan.Runtime.O3), schedules
        (armed schedules with `{ dueAt }` or `{ left }`, never both, and `pausedBy` beside `left`; keyed schedules as
        keyed lists) and operations (the operations' rows, @plan.Operations.O6; s.operations.<instance>.<name> for a
        fragment, a keyed list of copies under .each, each write row listing the lanes of that copy's path).

        Every list of several things is a keyed list in key order, each entry with key; an entry that does not exist is
        absent, never a default. Outside snapshot and view types omit Flow.private fields; Stories and Inspection still
        see them. It has no input, methods or public revision.

        Diagnostic values are @plan.Diagnostics.O1. Snapshots are deeply frozen plain data. A Runtime step publishes the
        bound lane rows it changes as a new snapshot; reading an old snapshot never consults live lanes.

        Unchanged parts retain identity across publications; Flow-owned containers are copied, while opaque application
        values retain identity. A later success or release clears its own source's issue, never another source's issue;
        a new actor incarnation inherits none. Disposal is @plan.Runtime.O6.
verification
    V1  Type tests prove inference and reject undeclared or foreign members, invalid guards,
        unfilled required ports, writes to machine input fields and fragment acting members used outside their fragment.
    V2  Tests prove construction rejects invalid declarations in order without starting work,
        including TwoDefaults, ImpureDefault, ReservedStateName and UnknownNotUncertain.
    V3  Stories prove self, re-entry as a new activation, descendant survival, parent/child work
        order, subroutine exits and abandonment, and history.
    V4  Tests prove the turn algorithm: before and after memory reads, atomic batches, fixed now,
        no-ops, refusal by a child and callback defect behavior, props in every memory reader, emit sources and an emit
        mapper's pre-commit defect, a props-only turn, and the .each fan-out raise (skip, deny, no copies).
    V5  Tests prove check agrees with handling and uses captured snapshots without effects.
    V6  Stories prove schedule order, armOn and disarmOn, keyed schedules, cancellation, debounce,
        overdue firing, waiting while unused and restore behavior, armOn tokens, `armOn: [Flow.entry]` beside restartOn,
        per-key durations, paused (hold, resume, keyed, overdue, multi-step, lane-only resume, restore re-evaluation)
        and a computed negative delay.
    V7  Stories prove placement retention, release, the owner test, late turns and passive lane
        publication.
    V8  Stories prove reaction baselines, context triggers, event order and defect recovery, onProps baselines and
        memory repair.
    V9  Tests prove frozen captured snapshots, stable identities and source-specific issue clearing, props and
        `{ left }` in frozen snapshots.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Internal identity encoding and construction validation machinery. Warning presentation. Schedule stepping
        implementation and inspection status fields. Issue indexing and diagnostic rendering details. Type-spike sizing
        and compiler performance measurements. Reflection behavior for proxies, getters, symbols and sparse arrays.
